= Unit 7 · Multilinear algebra

== Lecture 13 · Exterior powers and alternating maps
#focus[Construct exterior powers through a universal property and derive their basis in the free case.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall alternating means vanishing on repeated inputs, including in characteristic two.


A multilinear map is alternating if it vanishes whenever two inputs are equal. This definition works in every characteristic. It implies that interchanging two inputs changes the sign: expand the zero value with those two slots both equal to $x+y$. In characteristic two, the sign disappears, but the vanishing condition remains stronger than a bare symmetry statement.

#definition[Exterior power][The module $wedge^n M$ is the quotient of the $n$-fold tensor product by the submodule generated by pure tensors having equal inputs in two positions. The class of $m_1 otimes dots otimes m_n$ is written $m_1 wedge dots wedge m_n$. Set $wedge^0 M=R$.]

#strategy[Use multilinearity for spanning and alternating coordinate detectors for independence.]

#theorem[Exterior universal property][Alternating $n$-linear maps $M^n -> P$ correspond uniquely to linear maps $wedge^n M -> P$.]
#proof[The repeated tensor universal property first corresponds to multilinear maps on $M^n$. Such a map descends through the indicated quotient exactly when it vanishes on the generators with repeated inputs, which is the alternating condition. The quotient factorization is unique.]

#theorem[Exterior basis][If $M$ is free with ordered basis $e_1,dots,e_r$, then $wedge^n M$ is free with basis $e_(i_1)wedge dots wedge e_(i_n)$ for $i_1<dots<i_n$. It is zero for $n>r$ and has rank $binom(r,n)$ otherwise.]
#strategy[Expand to prove spanning, then construct coordinate detectors to prove independence.]
#proof[Multilinearity expands any wedge into basis wedges. Repeated indices give zero, and interchanging adjacent inputs lets us sort the remaining indices with the appropriate sign, proving spanning. For each increasing index set $I$, define the alternating form that takes the determinant of the matrix of the $I$-coordinates of the inputs. It induces a linear functional on the exterior power. On a sorted basis wedge it is one for the matching index set and zero for every other set. These coordinate functionals prove independence over any commutative ring.]

#example[Area as an algebraic coefficient][In $R^2$, $(a e_1+b e_2)wedge(c e_1+d e_2)=(a d-b c)e_1 wedge e_2$. This is an identity of modules; when $R=RR$, the coefficient also measures signed area. The algebraic definition does not require a metric.]

An endomorphism $T:M -> M$ induces $wedge^n T$ by applying $T$ to each input. Alternation ensures this descends, and the formula immediately gives functoriality: exterior powers preserve identity and composition.

#example[An exterior coefficient computed twice][In $R^3$, take $u=e_1+e_2$ and $v=e_2+e_3$. Expansion gives $u wedge v=e_1 wedge e_2+e_1 wedge e_3+e_2 wedge e_3$. The three coefficients also equal the two-by-two minors formed from the relevant pairs of coordinates of $u,v$. These coordinate forms detect the three independent basis wedges.]

Over a field, $u wedge v=0$ exactly when the two vectors are dependent: if $u$ is nonzero, extend it to a basis and inspect the coefficients of $u wedge v$. Over a ring, dependence and rank require more care, so this field conclusion should not be transferred without hypotheses.

#checkpoint("7.1", [Why does $e_1 wedge e_2+e_2 wedge e_1=0$ hold without dividing by two?], [Expand $(e_1+e_2)wedge(e_1+e_2)=0$ and use the two repeated-input terms being zero.])
#takeaways[The exterior construction is determined by alternating maps. Coordinate determinants prove the basis theorem rather than serving as an unexplained definition.]
