== Lecture 14 · Intrinsic trace and determinant
#focus[Define trace without selecting a basis, define determinant through the top exterior power, and derive invariance and multiplicativity.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall a finite projective module has a finite dual basis even when it has no ordinary basis.


For a finite free module of rank $n$, $wedge^n M$ is a free rank-one module. Every endomorphism of it is multiplication by a unique scalar, independent of a chosen generator.

#definition[Determinant][For finite free $M$ of rank $n$, define $det(T)$ by $wedge^n T=det(T)1_(wedge^n M)$. In a basis this is exactly the coefficient of $e_1 wedge dots wedge e_n$ in $T e_1 wedge dots wedge T e_n$.]

#strategy[Define determinant on the top exterior power; for trace, expand one dual basis in another and interchange finite sums.]

#theorem[Determinant laws][The determinant is independent of basis, $det(T S)=det(T)det(S)$, and $det(1)=1$.]
#proof[The definition is made on the intrinsic module $wedge^n M$, so changing a basis only changes a generator of this rank-one module by a unit and does not change the scalar of its endomorphism. Functoriality gives $wedge^n(T S)=(wedge^n T)(wedge^n S)$, hence multiplication of their scalars. The identity law follows the same way. Expanding the wedge in a basis gives the usual signed-permutation formula.]

To extend trace beyond free modules, let $M$ be finite projective. Since it is a summand of a finite free module, there are elements $m_i in M$ and linear functionals $phi_i:M -> R$ with $m=sum_i phi_i(m)m_i$ for every $m$. This is a dual basis, not necessarily an ordinary basis: take standard vectors in the containing free module, project them to $M$, and restrict the coordinate functionals to $M$.

#definition[Trace on a finite projective module][Define $"tr"(T)=sum_i phi_i(T m_i)$ using any finite dual basis.]
#theorem[Independence and cyclicity][This trace is independent of the dual basis and satisfies $"tr"(T S)="tr"(S T)$.]
#proof[For another dual basis $n_j,psi_j$, expand $m_i=sum_j psi_j(m_i)n_j$. Then $sum_i phi_i(T m_i)=sum_(i,j)psi_j(m_i)phi_i(T n_j)=sum_j psi_j(T n_j)$, using the first dual-basis identity on $T n_j$. This proves independence. For cyclicity expand $S m_i$ in the dual basis to get $"tr"(T S)=sum_(i,j)phi_j(S m_i)phi_i(T m_j)$. Commutativity of $R$ and interchanging $i,j$ give the corresponding formula for $"tr"(S T)$.]

For a finite locally free module of constant rank $n$, the determinant line is $det(M)=wedge^n M$, a locally free rank-one module, and the intrinsic determinant of $T$ is the induced endomorphism of this line. On every localization where $M$ has a basis it is described by the usual determinant scalar, and changing that basis does not alter the scalar. This formulation remains meaningful when a global basis does not exist.

#example[An operator and a cyclic representation][A representation of a cyclic group of order $n$ on $V$ is a linear operator $T$ satisfying $T^n=1$. It is a module over $K[t] slash(t^n-1)$. Trace and determinant of $T$ are independent of the chosen coordinate description, and $det(T)^n=1$.]
#example[Trace as a rank-one contraction][For $T(m)=phi(m)v$ and dual basis $(m_i,psi_i)$, the trace is $sum_i phi(m_i)psi_i(v)$. Since $v=sum_i psi_i(v)m_i$, linearity of $phi$ makes this sum $phi(v)$. No global basis of $M$ was required.]

This formula is a useful consistency test for an intrinsic definition: in coordinates it becomes the familiar sum of diagonal entries of an outer product. The dual-basis proof explains why the same scalar is obtained after changing the finite family used to reconstruct elements. Independence is proved, not assumed because the expression looks coordinate-like.

#checkpoint("7.2", [Why does replacing a matrix by $P^(-1)A P$ preserve trace and determinant?], [Use cyclicity of trace and multiplicativity of determinant, together with the inverse identities for $P$.])
#takeaways[Trace contracts an endomorphism against a dual basis; determinant measures its action on a top exterior power. Both are structural, not coordinate-dependent recipes.]
